breaks, the ground bears thorns. Genesis 5: eight times “and he died,” with Noah (“rest/comfort”) as exception. Genesis 6: “it repented the LORD… it grieved Him” (6:6), but “Noah found grace (chen) in the eyes of the LORD” (6:8). Haftarah (Machar Chodesh): “tomorrow is the new moon”; Jonathan’s covenant with David across the parting (“the LORD between us… for ever”). Gospel: the lights go out (Matt. 24:29), “heaven and earth shall pass away, my words shall not” (24:35), the hour known only to the Father (24:36).</p>
  <h3>0.2 The four axes as an open question</h3>
  <p>Does the weight fall on what God is and does (Source: the creating, remaining word), on the movement through time (Trajectory: beginning → fall → death → grace → end, and the renewal of the moon), on the wholeness of gift/grasp (Coherence: receiving vs grasping), or on the response (Will: hearing, abiding)?</p>
  <h3>0.3 The centre of gravity read off</h3>
  <p>🟢 Load-bearing are <strong>Source</strong> and <strong>Trajectory</strong>: God speaks the world into being (gift), and that same word remains when the creation passes away (Matt. 24:35). Centre of gravity: the word that remains — from “in the beginning… and God said” to “my words shall not pass away,” with the gift of creation, the grasp of the fall, and an ending in grace (6:8).</p>
  <h3>0.4 Framing test</h3>
  <p>🔴 Guarded. The weekly reflex (receiving vs grasping) is here not imposed but text-central: creation is the primal gift, the fall the primal grasp (“she took”). But the centre does not stay on that axis; the real deepening is the word-as-constant (Trajectory, Matt. 24:35). Guard: John 1:1-3 (the Logos in creation) and Matt. 24 are confessional horizon, not peshat-setter; the “speaking” in Genesis 1 is God’s word, not simply a doctrine of the Trinity.</p>

  <h2>§1 The thread</h2>
  <p>The world exists by a spoken word; it hangs on the Speaker, not on itself. So everything is gift before it is achievement. Man grasps what he was given to receive, and brings in death; God is grieved, and yet grace is found. And when at the end the lights go out and heaven and earth pass away, the word that called them remains.</p>

  <h2>§2 Peshat per block</h2>
  <table>
  <tr><th>Block</th><th>What it says (peshat)</th><th>Keyword / contrast</th><th>Open question</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Gen. 1:1-5</td><td>“In the beginning God created”; “and God said, Let there be light”; light divided from darkness.</td><td>bara · and God said</td><td>Why does He create by speaking?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gen. 1:14-19</td><td>The lights on day 4, “for signs and for seasons”.</td><td>light before the lights · moadim</td><td>Why light before sun and moon?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gen. 1:26-31</td><td>Man in God’s image; all “very good”; the garden given.</td><td>image · very good · gift</td><td>What does the one prohibition mean?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gen. 3:1-24</td><td>“Hath God said?”; “she took… and ate”; shame, blame, thorns, expulsion.</td><td>taking ↔ receiving</td><td>What breaks first?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gen. 4–5</td><td>Cain and Abel; the genealogy with eight times “and he died”; Noah = rest.</td><td>“and he died” · rest</td><td>What does the rhythm of death say?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gen. 6:1-8</td><td>Violence; “it repented the LORD… it grieved Him”; “Noah found grace”.</td><td>grief · grace (chen)</td><td>How does God’s sorrow stand beside grace?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1 Sam. 20:18-42</td><td>“Tomorrow is the new moon”; Jonathan’s loyalty above his throne; parting and covenant.</td><td>new moon · covenant remains</td><td>What remains when the ways divide?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Matt. 24:29-36</td><td>Sun/moon/stars go out; the Son of Man; “heaven and earth pass, my words not”; the hour known only to the Father.</td><td>lights go out · word remains</td><td>On what do you hang when the made perishes?</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>§3 Two load-bearing axes</h2>
  <table>
  <tr><th>Axis</th><th>Text anchors</th><th>Diagnosis</th><th>Open anomaly</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Source</td><td>1:3 · 1:14 · 6:8 · Matt. 24:35</td><td>Load-bearing. God creates and carries by His word; that word remains as the creation passes; the ending is grace.</td><td>Why light before the light-sources?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Trajectory</td><td>1:1 → 3:6 → 5 (“and he died”) → 6:8 → Matt. 24:29-35</td><td>Load-bearing, second pole. From beginning to fall to death to grace to end; and the moon that wanes and renews.</td><td>Is the end de-creation or fulfilment?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Coherence</td><td>1:29-30 (gift) ↔ 3:6 (grasp) · 1 Sam. 20:42 (covenant remains)</td><td>Supporting. Gift and grasp are the two basic postures; the covenant word stands across the parting.</td><td>Where does “receiving” become grasping again?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Will</td><td>3:6 (choosing) · Matt. 24:42 (watch)</td><td>Response, not source: hearing and abiding, or grasping and falling.</td><td>Is watching reckoning or trusting?</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>§4 Sources round</h2>
  <p>Van de Giessen covers Genesis 1–25; the rabbinic witnesses were verified via Sefaria.</p>
  <p>🟢 <strong>Van de Giessen (pre-peshat).</strong> Bara (1:1) is used in the Bible only for God’s creating; cross-refs to John 1:1-3 and Heb. 11:3 (the world by the word). The light of day 1 (1:3) precedes the lights of day 4 — an old crux he discusses without forcing it. At 1:14 he notes the lights “for signs and seasons.” And Noah (6) means “rest/comfort,” a counter-note in the register of death.</p>
  <p>🟢 <strong>Rashi on 1:14.</strong> “Signs” points to the renewal of the moon — the lights govern the appointed times and the new moon. That ties the parashah directly to the Machar Chodesh haftarah.</p>
  <p>🟡 <strong>Ibn Ezra on 1:14.</strong> “Signs” may also point to hours and to lunar and solar eclipses — the lights as a measurable order, not as gods. Chizkuni places the lights in the firmament. Illustrative.</p>
  <p>🔵 <strong>John 1:1-3 / Matthew 24 (confessional).</strong> The creating word of Genesis 1 is confessed as the Logos who “in the beginning was with God” and became flesh; and “my words shall not pass away” (24:35) continues the enduring-word motif. Horizon, not peshat-setter.</p>
  <p>🟢 <strong>De-creation in the prophets.</strong> Jeremiah 4:23 and Isaiah 34:11 use the image of tohu va-vohu for the consequence of sin: the creation order is dismantled, the light taken away; Isaiah 45:18 says the opposite — God did not create the earth a tohu but to be inhabited. Precision note (set by the author): the “emptiness of sin” has its own roots (hevel, shav, awen, riq) and is linguistically not tohu/bohu; the link is thematic/theological, not lexical. Cassuto reads tohu as clay on the potter’s wheel — raw, waiting, not moral chaos.</p>
  <p>🟢 <strong>Genesis 3:16 ↔ 4:7 (grammatical mirror).</strong> The same syntax with teshuqah (desire) and mashal (rule): to Eve “he shall rule over you,” to Cain “you must rule over it” (timshal-bo). God reverses the axis: Cain is addressed on his moral sovereignty over the lurking drive. Teshuqah occurs in the whole Tanakh only 3× (Gen. 3:16; 4:7; Song 7:10).</p>
  <p>🟢/🟡 <strong>Genesis 4:8 (text-critical gap).</strong> The MT has “and Cain spoke to his brother Abel” without the direct speech; SP, LXX, Vulgate, Peshitta and Targum add “let us go into the field.” Three readings: scribal omission (homoioteleuton around “the field”), harmonisation by the translators, or deliberate aposiopesis — the omission then stressing that Cain’s words were mere pretext to lure him away from the witnesses.</p>
  <p>🟢 <strong>Jonathan = “the LORD has given” (Yehonathan).</strong> The name lays bare the contrast with Cain: grasping versus gift. Jonathan dismantles his dynastic claim and preserves the anointed (hesed); the empty place at Saul’s table (1 Sam. 20:18,25) stands against the covenant that remains whole (20:42). Two questions frame the parashah: “where are you?” (3:9, the Creator) and “where is your brother?” (4:9, the neighbour).</p>
  <p>⚪ <strong>Onkelos</strong> on 1:1/1:3: still to be verified.</p>
  <p><strong>Revision gate.</strong> Did the round shift the centre? Confirming: bara-as-God’s-act-alone and “signs = new moon” (Rashi) strengthen Source/Trajectory and knot the three readings on one thread — the word that creates, sets the times, and remains.</p>

  <h2>§5 Direction test as horizon</h2>
  <p>🔵 Confessional. The word by which God created “became flesh and dwelt among us” in Yeshua (John 1:14) — here, and only here, accommodation applies. And when in Matthew 24 the fourth-day lights go out, His word remains: whoever abides in it, abides. 🔴 Guarded: Matt. 24 and John 1 do not settle the peshat of Genesis; “God said” is first of all God’s creating speech.</p>

  <h2>§6 Status table</h2>
  <table>
  <tr><th>Claim</th><th>Status</th></tr>
  <tr><td>God creates by speaking; bara only for God’s act (1:1,3)</td><td>🟢 load-bearing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Light (day 1) before the lights (day 4); lights “for signs and seasons” (1:14)</td><td>🟢 load-bearing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>“Signs” = renewal of the moon (Rashi) — tie to the Machar Chodesh haftarah</td><td>🟢 load-bearing (verified)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>PLACEHOLDER_EN (“she took and ate,” 3:6); death as rhythm (“and he died,” 5)</td><td>🟢 load-bearing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>God’s grief (6:6) and grace (chen, 6:8) — the ending is grace, not judgment</td><td>🟢 load-bearing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reflex-landing on receiving/grasping without the word-constant</td><td>🔴 guardrail</td></tr>
  <tr><td>The creating word confessed as the Logos (John 1); “my words shall not pass” (Matt. 24:35)</td><td>🔵 confessional</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Onkelos on 1:1/1:3; Ibn Ezra “signs = eclipses”</td><td>⚪/🟡 verification/suggestive</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>§7 Podcast rendering</h2>
  <p>One physical image: a child in a pitch-dark room flipping the light switch — and wanting to do it a hundred times, to pause over one wonder: there can be light, because He spoke it. And beside it the water and sand you mix and then watch separate again: creating is making separation, making clarity. So the Word works in a confused mind — it divides and clarifies, and it stands even when all the lights go out.</p>

  <h2>§8 Source accountability</h2>
  <p>Van de Giessen, Notes on Genesis (1–25): bara (1:1), light day 1 vs lights day 4, 1:14 “signs/seasons,” Noah = rest — from the file. Rashi and Ibn Ezra on 1:14, Chizkuni — verified via Sefaria. John 1:1-3,14 and Matt. 24:29-36 confessional (🔵). Onkelos: still to be verified. Name: Yeshua. Author: Arie Alberts.</p>
